<!-- This file is the source. Edit it, then run vignettes/precompute.R with a WildObs API
key to rebuild images.Rmd, which ships with its results already in place. -->

Classifying camera trap images with machine learning requires balanced datasets of 
representative images for each known species. WildObs records the species in every image
of an observation, so its openly hosted images come labelled. This vignette finds those 
images, joins them to their labels, and downloads a small batch. It uses a 2023 
University of Queensland teaching survey at Dwyers Scrub Conservation Park, Queensland, where
a curated selection of images is hosted by the Tagged Image Repository of WildObs and the Atlas
of Living Australia ([ALA](https://collections.ala.org.au/public/show/dr31594)). 

```{r download, message = FALSE}
# load the R package 
library(WildObsR)

# your personal API key, read from .Renviron (see the Get started vignette)
api_key <- Sys.getenv("WILDOBSR_API_KEY")

# specify the project to access 
id <- "QLD_Dwyers_Scrub_ANIM3018_2023_WildObsID_0005"
# download the data package, while including the media resource, and take the first value in the list
dp <- wildobs_dp_download(api_key = api_key, project_ids = id, media = TRUE)[[id]]
# extract the media table from the data package 
media <- frictionless::read_resource(dp, "media")
```

## Which images can be downloaded

Most WildObs images stay with the people who collected them. However, WildObs curates 
a selection of images from openly shared projects based on the number of detections
within a project (*e.g.*, species with few detections retain all images, while species
with many detections retain an image per detection). Only images with `filePublic = TRUE`
are openly hosted by WildObs or the ALA, and only those can be downloaded. The rest are
listed, with their timestamps and labels, but their `filePath` reads 
`not_publicly_accessible`, and file names are withheld from public users.

```{r public}
# inspect how many images in this project are hosted openly
table(media$filePublic)
```

Across the whole public database the share of openly hosted images is small, so check
`filePublic` before planning a training set.

## Label each image with its species

Each media row carries the `observationID` it belongs to, a WildObs addition to Camtrap DP.
Joining on it gives every image the species, count and observation type recorded for that
observation:

```{r labels}
# extract the observations table, read with their schema types
obs <- frictionless::read_resource(dp, "observations")
# specify the label columns of each observation
labels <- obs[, c("observationID", "observationType", "scientificName", "count")]

# merge the relevant observation fields to each public image with its label
public <- merge(media[media$filePublic, c("mediaID", "deploymentID", "observationID",
                                          "timestamp", "filePath")],
                labels, by = "observationID")

# view the species with the most openly hosted images
head(sort(table(public$scientificName[public$observationType == "animal"]),
          decreasing = TRUE), 8)
```

Subset to the species you need straight away. A project's openly hosted images may
occasionally include records that do not belong in a training set, such as misidentified
blanks or images of people, and working with one species keeps them out. Here, the 
Australian brush-turkey (*Alectura lathami*):

```{r species}
# the openly hosted images of brush-turkeys only
turkey <- public[public$observationType == "animal" &
                   public$scientificName %in% "Alectura lathami", ]
# how many images do we have?
nrow(turkey)
```

Projects also differ in whether they host blank images (`observationType == "blank"`).
This one does; keep some in a training set, because models must also learn to say that
nothing is there.

## Download a batch

The function `wildobs_media_download()` saves each file into a folder per project and 
deployment, skips files already on disk, and reports what happened to each one. Start 
with a few files to check the setup before downloading thousands:

```{r batch}
# start with six brush-turkey images, as a test batch
batch <- utils::head(turkey, 6)

# specify the folder where the images will be saved (temporary directory)
out_dir <- file.path(tempdir(), "wildobs_images")
# and download them into that folder
result <- wildobs_media_download(batch, out_dir = out_dir)

# check what happened to each file, and where it went
table(result$downloadStatus)
# what are the file names of the images saved to your computer
basename(result$localPath)
```

The returned table keeps every column you passed in, so the labels stay with the files:
`result$scientificName` names the species in each `result$localPath`. Write it out with
the images, for example as `labels.csv`, and the batch is ready for training.

## Cite and credit

Images are licensed with their project by a Creative Commons licence that requires
attribution. Check the media licence, and cite the project in any model or dataset
you publish:

```{r licence}
# the licence that applies to this project's images
extract_metadata(dp, "licenses")[, c("name", "scope", "path")]

# and the reference to cite
writeLines(cite_datapackages(dp))
```

